# Component-Based Entity Architecture

## Context
- When creating complex game entities with multiple behaviors
- When code reuse across different entity types is important
- When entity behavior needs to be modified at runtime
- When working on medium to large Phaser game projects

## Requirements
- Extend Phaser.GameObjects with component-based design
- Separate entity behaviors into individual components
- Design components to be reusable across different entity types
- Ensure components can be added/removed dynamically
- Components should follow single responsibility principle

## Examples

<example>
```typescript
// Component interface compatible with Phaser's lifecycle
interface Component {
  update(time: number, delta: number): void;
  destroy(): void;
}

// Base entity class that integrates with Phaser's GameObject system
class Entity extends Phaser.GameObjects.Container {
  private components: Map<string, Component> = new Map();

  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene, x: number, y: number) {
    super(scene, x, y);
    scene.add.existing(this);
    scene.sys.updateList.add(this);
  }

  addComponent(name: string, component: Component): this {
    this.components.set(name, component);
    return this;
  }

  getComponent<T extends Component>(name: string): T | undefined {
    return this.components.get(name) as T;
  }

  removeComponent(name: string): boolean {
    const component = this.components.get(name);
    if (component) {
      component.destroy();
      return this.components.delete(name);
    }
    return false;
  }

  update(time: number, delta: number): void {
    // Update all components
    this.components.forEach(component => component.update(time, delta));
  }

  destroy(fromScene?: boolean): void {
    // Clean up all components
    this.components.forEach(component => component.destroy());
    this.components.clear();
    super.destroy(fromScene);
  }
}

// Basic usage
const createEnemy = (scene: Phaser.Scene, x: number, y: number): Entity => {
  const enemy = new Entity(scene, x, y);
  
  // Add sprite as a child of the container
  const sprite = scene.add.sprite(0, 0, 'enemy');
  enemy.add(sprite);
  
  return enemy;
};
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```typescript
// Monolithic entity class with mixed responsibilities
class Enemy extends Phaser.GameObjects.Sprite {
  private health: number = 100;
  private speed: number = 150;
  private attackDamage: number = 10;
  
  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene, x: number, y: number) {
    super(scene, x, y, 'enemy');
    scene.add.existing(this);
    scene.physics.world.enable(this);
  }
  
  update(): void {
    // Movement, attacks, health, and animations all mixed together
    // No separation of concerns
    // Hard to reuse code across different entity types
  }
  
  // Many methods handling different aspects of behavior
  // Tightly coupled and difficult to modify
}
```
This monolithic class mixes multiple responsibilities, making it difficult to extend, test, or reuse. Adding new features requires modifying the entire class, increasing complexity and the risk of bugs.
</example>

## Phaser-Specific Implementation Tips

1. Use Phaser.GameObjects.Container as a base class for entities:
   - Containers can hold multiple child GameObjects
   - Allows grouping visual elements with component logic
   - Provides consistent positioning for all components

2. Integrate with Phaser's update loop:
   ```typescript
   // Add entity to update list
   scene.sys.updateList.add(entityInstance);
   
   // Remove when no longer needed
   scene.sys.updateList.remove(entityInstance);
   ```

3. For physics entities, add the physics body to the container:
   ```typescript
   // Enable physics on the container
   scene.physics.world.enable(entityContainer);
   
   // Access the body for physics operations
   const body = entityContainer.body as Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Body;
   ```

